{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Let me cross over now and see, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of his saying <b>now</b> (נָא), and also why it was necessary for him to say <b>and I will see</b>, etc. — for when he crosses over, the matter itself implies that he will see, and so forth. Perhaps it was intended to provide an answer to the matters that prevent his entry into the land, as they said, of blessed memory, that there are two: first, because the time of Joshua had arrived to rule (Tanchuma), and one sovereignty does not touch its fellow even as much as a hair's breadth; second, because the Holy One, blessed be He, foresaw that Israel were destined to sin and He would need to pour out His wrath upon them, and HaShem considered making for them, for their sake, the Temple, as hinted in the doubled word משכן משכן, as they said, of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 51), and in this there is hope and a good outcome for Israel, as they said, of blessed memory (Midrash Tehillim 79:1), on the verse ״מִזְמוֹר לְאָסָף אֱלֹהִים בָּאוּ גוֹיִם״ etc. — מזמור, a lamentation (קינה) is what he should have said, except that He poured out His wrath upon wood and stone and did not pour it out upon Israel, etc. — end quote. And if Moses had built the Temple, they said, of blessed memory (Sotah 9a), no nation or tongue would have held sway over it, and this would cause Him to pour out His wrath upon them to annihilate them, as I explained in parashat Devarim on the verse (Deuteronomy 1:37) ״גַּם בִּי הִתְאַנַּף״." "<b>For this reason</b> Moses intended in his words to overcome these two matters. Against the sovereignty that does not touch [its fellow], etc., he said <b>let me cross over now</b> (אֶעְבְּרָה נָא) — meaning, let me cross over in the manner of those who pass through, not in a manner of rulership and glory. And against the concern for the people of Israel, so that they would have the Temple as a ransom for their souls, he said <b>and I will see</b> (וְאֶרֶאֶה) — meaning, not to build, but only to see. And by this both concerns were removed. And he was precise in saying the word <b>now</b> (נָא) — meaning, let him now pass from his sovereignty in order to attain this hope." "<b>Further</b>, he intended to request that He hasten his crossing while there is still time in the day, before his days were yet completed. And according to what I wrote in my commentary on ״at that time״ (בָּעֵת הַהִיא) — that it refers to the time of the decree of the spies — he intended to say that he would hasten to cross in this time when his days were still many, and from within these matters you will understand that he intended to nullify the decree against Israel, for their decree was that they would die in the wilderness and would not die the death of excision until they were all sixty years old; and if Moses were to enter then, the matter is self-evident that all Israel would enter — for HaShem had not decreed upon them that they die the death of excision." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to their saying, of blessed memory (Bemidbar Rabbah 19:13), that the expected redeemer for the awaiting people is Moses himself, and in the Zohar (vol. 2, 120a) they also alluded to this in the verse ״מַה שֶּׁהָיָה הוּא שֶׁיִּהְיֶה״ (Ecclesiastes 1:9). And the prophet Moses knew these things, for HaShem revealed His secret to His faithful servant in His household, that a time would come afterward when he would cross over the Jordan into the land and complete [his mission]; therefore he was precise in his prayer, for what he asks is that he cross over now — and that is his saying <b>let me cross over now</b> (אֶעְבְּרָה נָא), at this time." "<b>Further</b>, it appears to me to explain his saying <b>let me cross over now</b> (אֶעְבְּרָה נָא), that he intended to respond also to the reason given in their words, of blessed memory (Devarim Rabbah), for why Moses died outside the land — namely, because of the dead of the wilderness, to bring them into the World to Come, a parable, etc. — let his words be examined there. Therefore he said <b>let me cross over</b> (אֶעְבְּרָה) — meaning, in a manner of crossing through, not to remain, but only let me cross with my feet; and when the time arrives that HaShem says to him, your days have drawn near to die, he would come to the place that HaShem would designate for him." "<b>Further,</b> he intends in the language of <b>let me cross over</b>, after the manner of their saying, may their memory be for blessing (Ketubot 111a): whoever walks four cubits in the land of Israel acquires a portion in the World to Come. Therefore he said <b>let me cross over now</b> — meaning, now while alive, for the benefit of my soul for the anticipated delight which is the World to Come." "<b>that is beyond the Jordan.</b> It was necessary to say thus, because there is a good land that is the supernal land, in the manner of the verse (Psalms 116:9): ״אֶתְהַלֵּךְ לִפְנֵי ה׳ בְּאַרְצוֹת הַחַיִּים,״ and it was necessary to specify the good mountain and the Lebanon, according to their words, may their memory be for blessing, who said (Sotah 14a) that Moses' intention was to fulfill the commandments that apply in the land. Therefore he specified all the places, to say that he desires to fulfill all the commandments that apply in every place." "Furthermore, Moses cleverly chose to say these things, so that there would be no opening for HaShem to say that He would show him the land from there and he would not need to cross over. Therefore he specified all the places, for there is no possibility of seeing all this from afar unless he crosses over:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:06.059363331Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}